00:12:56.700 --> 00:12:59.310
or are they brand driven organizations.

00:12:59.310 --> 00:13:00.563
So many people here, and with

00:13:00.563 --> 00:13:03.060
a great sponsor to
introduce me, I love sales,

00:13:03.060 --> 00:13:04.800
I love Google, Google's great.

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:06.480
Somebody's typing in they want a burger

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:09.120
in their local area, like
it's nice to show up,

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:11.010
it's good to show up.

00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:14.390
That's sales, let there be no confusion.

00:13:14.390 --> 00:13:15.940
There are so many people in this room

00:13:15.940 --> 00:13:19.220
that are wildly missing
the delta between a sales

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:20.840
and a marketing organization.

00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:23.170
And marketing and branding always gets

00:13:23.170 --> 00:13:27.020
destroyed by CFOS and
short term operators.

00:13:27.020 --> 00:13:29.080
Right, nobody I mean, this is why it's fun

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:31.410
to deliver keynotes like
this and have lights on,

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:33.930
seeing the reactions we all know.

00:13:33.930 --> 00:13:36.880
Nobody's got the patience
to let marketing play out.

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:40.350
We live in such a quaint, every 90 days,

00:13:40.350 --> 00:13:42.057
kind of environment.

00:13:42.057 --> 00:13:44.000
If you're lucky on 90 days.

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:46.720
The reality though is if
you look at the businesses,

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:49.180
the brands that people are baffled by,

00:13:49.180 --> 00:13:52.090
I see my man from Ann Pizza or
Kona Ice or all these things

00:13:52.090 --> 00:13:55.130
that people are just surprised
that it's doing so well.

00:13:55.130 --> 00:13:57.490
They are dramatically more brand driven

00:13:57.490 --> 00:13:59.800
than they are transactional.

00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:04.440
I mean I'm just fascinated by
our inability as a collective

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:07.510
to understand that and
create new ways to measure

00:14:07.510 --> 00:14:11.170
businesses, to create new
ways to measure success.

00:14:11.170 --> 00:14:15.550
So for me, the biggest
thing is very simple.

00:14:15.550 --> 00:14:18.040
We're living in a time where this device

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:21.730
controls our consumers' every move.

00:14:21.730 --> 00:14:23.870
You may not like it personally.

00:14:23.870 --> 00:14:26.400
I watch people every day
and executives every day

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:28.840
make business decisions based on how they

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:30.873
socially want the world to be.

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:34.260
I don't have a whole
lot of emotion that kids

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:35.853
don't go outside and play like we did,

00:14:35.853 --> 00:14:38.210
because we're old now.

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:40.050
Like that doesn't really drive me.

00:14:40.050 --> 00:14:43.180
I'm unemotional about
my ideological nirvana,

00:14:43.180 --> 00:14:44.860
of how the world should be.

00:14:44.860 --> 00:14:47.720
I operate, when I put my business suit on,

00:14:47.720 --> 00:14:49.810
which is not necessarily a suit.

00:14:49.810 --> 00:14:52.530
I operate based on the
reality of the consumer,

00:14:52.530 --> 00:14:55.870
not the way I want it to
be or what got me here

00:14:55.870 --> 00:14:57.490
over the last decade.

00:14:57.490 --> 00:15:00.330
My dad's liquor store
was built on direct mail

00:15:00.330 --> 00:15:02.980
and full page ads in the New York Times.

00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:05.200
Over my success people think
I did it with social media

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:07.780
and I did and it mattered post game,

00:15:07.780 --> 00:15:09.900
but I built a very large business,

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:12.120
I built just for context for
the business people room,

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:14.060
my dad's liquor store
was doing $3.3 million

00:15:14.060 --> 00:15:17.200
in revenue on 10% gross profit.

00:15:17.200 --> 00:15:20.470
Shoppers discount liquors, he meant it.

00:15:20.470 --> 00:15:21.593
Right?

00:15:21.593 --> 00:15:24.520
3.3 on 10% gross.

00:15:24.520 --> 00:15:26.740
I took that business in a six year period

00:15:26.740 --> 00:15:29.480
from that to a $62 million a year business

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:32.990
running on 27% gross profit,
without a credit line,

00:15:32.990 --> 00:15:36.670
and with no cash infusion
from BC, or private equity.

00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:37.660
How?

00:15:37.660 --> 00:15:41.420
I mean every marketing
penny work like a dollar.

00:15:41.420 --> 00:15:43.960
At first I figured out
how to do direct mail

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:46.220
in the way that I'm
telling you to do social.

00:15:46.220 --> 00:15:49.000
What I figured out as a child, in essence,

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:52.030
was that if I made seven different flyers

00:15:52.030 --> 00:15:54.000
that were more contextual to the addresses

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:55.670
in New Jersey that I was sending,

00:15:55.670 --> 00:15:58.180
that if I sent high end
wine to Short Hills,

00:15:58.180 --> 00:16:00.000
it would do better than if I sold

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.670
every day wine to Middlesex,
it would do better.

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:04.700
My cost of the creative was higher,

00:16:04.700 --> 00:16:07.210
and my dad and everybody
else made fun of me for it

00:16:07.210 --> 00:16:08.750
because you should pay
as little as possible

00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:11.430
for the print, the postage cost the most.

00:16:11.430 --> 00:16:14.020
But what I even understood
inherently then as a kid

00:16:14.020 --> 00:16:16.670
was that the context of
the creative mattered,

00:16:16.670 --> 00:16:18.730
not just the ability for them to see it.

00:16:18.730 --> 00:16:21.230
So my conversion rates
were dramatically bigger.

00:16:21.230 --> 00:16:25.590
That led me to the biggest
nirvana, which was in 1997

00:16:25.590 --> 00:16:28.030
I started a very
significant email newsletter

00:16:28.030 --> 00:16:29.540
because that was a new phenomenon.

00:16:29.540 --> 00:16:32.100
How many people here
were on email in 1997?

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:33.840
Raise your hands, just curious.

00:16:33.840 --> 00:16:36.070
So for the hands of the room,
for the youngsters in here,

00:16:36.070 --> 00:16:38.930
let me tell you about
us who had email in 97.

00:16:38.930 --> 00:16:41.133
We read every single email.

00:16:42.480 --> 00:16:43.340
Remember that?

00:16:43.340 --> 00:16:46.983
It was new, we read it, we
treated it like actual mail.

00:16:46.983 --> 00:16:50.120
How many people here are
close to their email marketing

00:16:50.120 --> 00:16:52.180
or have done email
marketing in their career?

00:16:52.180 --> 00:16:53.090
One more time.

00:16:53.090 --> 00:16:54.990
Great, ready for this, 90% open rates.

00:16:56.554 --> 00:16:57.704
90% open rates in 1997.

00:16:59.323 --> 00:17:01.950
bBt like I know the sun
will come up tomorrow

00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:04.750
I know that marketers ruin everything.

00:17:04.750 --> 00:17:06.890
And I spent the next decade ruining email

00:17:06.890 --> 00:17:09.340
and taking that from 90% to 30%,

00:17:09.340 --> 00:17:11.860
because you know at first it was five days

00:17:11.860 --> 00:17:13.310
a week email and that was incredible

00:17:13.310 --> 00:17:15.420
and then one miraculous
day a year or two in

00:17:15.420 --> 00:17:17.370
I was driving to the store and I'm like

00:17:17.370 --> 00:17:19.370
people read email on Saturday.

00:17:19.370 --> 00:17:20.920
And so we added the weekend,

00:17:20.920 --> 00:17:23.800
and then the breakdown fall of 2004,

00:17:23.800 --> 00:17:26.280
when I decided people
read email twice a day.

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:27.620
And we started emailing twice a day

00:17:27.620 --> 00:17:29.360
and you know exactly
where this story goes,

00:17:29.360 --> 00:17:32.170
and so learning the
pattern behavior with that,

00:17:32.170 --> 00:17:34.420
when Google AdWords came
out, the day it came out,

00:17:34.420 --> 00:17:36.880
I bought ads and I owned the word wine

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:39.620
for five cents a click, for a month

00:17:39.620 --> 00:17:42.540
before Google made a 10 cents floor,

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:45.080
and before anybody even knew it existed.

00:17:45.080 --> 00:17:47.400
Because I understood context,

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:49.880
I was then also good once everybody came

00:17:49.880 --> 00:17:53.570
in buying Silver Oak 1994
Napa Valley Cabernet,

00:17:53.570 --> 00:17:56.990
and those are my conversions
at five and 10 cents.

00:17:56.990 --> 00:17:59.810
That long tail contextual mentality

00:17:59.810 --> 00:18:02.560
is the biggest thing that's
missing from marketing today.

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:04.370
The number one reason most people in here

00:18:04.370 --> 00:18:06.510
are wasting most of their money is A,

00:18:06.510 --> 00:18:09.380
they're distributing it
somewhere people don't see,

00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:11.950
which is whether remnant TV direct mail,

00:18:11.950 --> 00:18:15.740
you know, print or see it
at a cost that's effective

00:18:15.740 --> 00:18:16.790
in comparison to their business,

00:18:16.790 --> 00:18:19.963
or two when they go
digital, they go vanilla.

00:18:20.830 --> 00:18:23.770
It is so easy to make a piece of creative

00:18:23.770 --> 00:18:26.010
that targets an Asian
American within a one mile

00:18:26.010 --> 00:18:29.630
radius of your store, and actually

00:18:29.630 --> 00:18:32.380
have Asian Americans in the creative.

00:18:32.380 --> 00:18:34.430
It is easy to contextualize your creative,

00:18:34.430 --> 00:18:35.930
you have to go in a different mindset,

00:18:35.930 --> 00:18:38.690
you have to put the
dollars into the creative

00:18:38.690 --> 00:18:40.440
not into the media amplification,

00:18:40.440 --> 00:18:42.850
that is against old best practices of the

00:18:42.850 --> 00:18:45.150
last 50 years of marketing,
but that's because

00:18:45.150 --> 00:18:46.380
the last 50 years of marketing were

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:48.150
a television down mentality,

00:18:48.150 --> 00:18:51.460
and now we're an internet,
consumer first up mentality,

00:18:51.460 --> 00:18:55.570
we must create relevance,
not potential reach.

00:18:55.570 --> 00:18:59.050
We must create relevance,
not potential reach,

00:18:59.050 --> 00:19:00.730
you have to tell me why.

00:19:00.730 --> 00:19:04.230
You put something about the
New York Jets in your creative,

00:19:04.230 --> 00:19:05.830
I'm going to eat your hamburger.

00:19:07.880 --> 00:19:09.630
You mentioned something
that we beat the Cowboys,

00:19:09.630 --> 00:19:11.640
I'm in a real good mood, right?

00:19:11.640 --> 00:19:13.440
Today, contextual.

00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:15.610
The speed in which that kind of creative,

00:19:15.610 --> 00:19:17.430
to get consumers to do something is

00:19:17.430 --> 00:19:20.550
not inherently native to this industry.

00:19:20.550 --> 00:19:22.320
As a matter of fact, it's not inherently

00:19:22.320 --> 00:19:24.930
native the most industries,
except the emerging direct

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:29.030
to consumer brands that live
mainly on Instagram today.

00:19:29.030 --> 00:19:31.640
Now, those businesses
are vulnerable because

00:19:31.640 --> 00:19:33.500
they've raised so much capital,

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:35.410
they're willing to
overpay for their customer

00:19:35.410 --> 00:19:36.800
and they're actually underwater,

00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:40.060
something that most of us
don't have the luxury for.

00:19:40.060 --> 00:19:41.980
But because of that they become lazy

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:45.160
and they also do not make
enough contextual creative,

00:19:45.160 --> 00:19:47.570
because they can afford to buy somebody

00:19:47.570 --> 00:19:49.350
that's worth less to them.

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:50.413
We cannot.

00:19:51.420 --> 00:19:54.420
However, what really is fascinating about

00:19:54.420 --> 00:19:57.150
actually taking this
consumer centric approach

00:19:57.150 --> 00:19:59.920
is this doesn't just matter in marketing.

00:19:59.920 --> 00:20:04.810
For example, I'm dying
to know in this industry,

00:20:04.810 --> 00:20:06.780
when it's going to figure out that every

00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:08.790
late afternoon and every evening

00:20:08.790 --> 00:20:12.397
it has a very interesting
expiring piece of inventory

00:20:12.397 --> 00:20:16.270
that can be converted in
massive customer acquisition.

00:20:16.270 --> 00:20:18.980
If I owned one of the
businesses in this room,

00:20:18.980 --> 00:20:21.000
the plan that I would have around food

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:24.700
that we throw out as
an offense to surprise

00:20:24.700 --> 00:20:27.070
and delight my local
community and customers

00:20:27.070 --> 00:20:29.770
and record those surprises and delights,

00:20:29.770 --> 00:20:33.810
and amplify that creative
would be at the top

00:20:33.810 --> 00:20:35.370
of the things that I would do.

00:20:35.370 --> 00:20:37.420
You're talking about an asset,

00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:39.420
you're talking about an asset.

00:20:39.420 --> 00:20:40.970
What we don't have as a mentality

00:20:40.970 --> 00:20:44.360
of creating and campaigning
and making ideas live,

00:20:44.360 --> 00:20:46.750
fast, recorded, and amplified.

00:20:46.750 --> 00:20:49.360
But we live in a world
where everybody in this room

00:20:49.360 --> 00:20:52.480
has an asset that is expiring every night,

00:20:52.480 --> 00:20:55.300
and having the speed in
which turning that asset

00:20:55.300 --> 00:20:58.320
into a surprise and delight
moment without undermining

00:20:58.320 --> 00:21:00.890
the core product, so
that people wait for it

00:21:00.890 --> 00:21:04.200
is an incredibly delicate
and important strategy

00:21:04.200 --> 00:21:06.500
that I highly recommend people debate on.

00:21:06.500 --> 00:21:08.800
Very simply to make it literal,

00:21:08.800 --> 00:21:11.860
taking whatever product
you sell and driving it

00:21:11.860 --> 00:21:14.160
somewhere that evening
and giving the high school

00:21:14.160 --> 00:21:15.950
football team the product before the game

00:21:15.950 --> 00:21:18.800
and filming that reaction and
then posting it on Facebook

00:21:18.800 --> 00:21:21.360
and targeting the local
area about that moment

00:21:21.360 --> 00:21:23.750
becomes the emotional
trigger that makes somebody

00:21:23.750 --> 00:21:27.053
consider your place that hadn't before.

00:21:28.351 --> 00:21:29.184
It matters.

00:21:30.410 --> 00:21:32.850
The other thing I'm
fascinated by is menu items.

00:21:32.850 --> 00:21:35.320
I'm fascinated by the math around that

00:21:35.320 --> 00:21:38.800
first item that drops
off, that isn't core,

00:21:38.800 --> 00:21:40.040
that you're always trying
to figure out if we

00:21:40.040 --> 00:21:41.780
can get one more item to the core,

00:21:41.780 --> 00:21:43.340
what that means to our economics

00:21:43.340 --> 00:21:45.330
and the ability to create
marketing around that

00:21:45.330 --> 00:21:47.840
sixth, seventh, eighth item.

00:21:47.840 --> 00:21:50.090
When you go away from making one vanilla

00:21:50.090 --> 00:21:52.700
brand piece of content
to a world of thousands,

00:21:52.700 --> 00:21:55.910
all of a sudden your onion rings strategy

00:21:55.910 --> 00:21:57.983
creatively becomes a real game.

00:21:59.190 --> 00:22:02.533
Very, very, very, important,
very, very opportunistic.

00:22:04.010 --> 00:22:07.070
This leads me down a path that
I'd like to get the people

00:22:07.070 --> 00:22:09.810
in this room to think about
which is the following.

00:22:09.810 --> 00:22:13.220
If you do not understand
that you are a media company

00:22:13.220 --> 00:22:16.510
first, comma, then a fast casual,

00:22:16.510 --> 00:22:19.820
then a QSR, then a restaurant business,

00:22:19.820 --> 00:22:21.510
you will become very vulnerable.

00:22:21.510 --> 00:22:23.680
I believe what you're
seeing happen in society,

00:22:23.680 --> 00:22:27.140
politics, social issues, everything,

00:22:27.140 --> 00:22:29.350
it's happening in front of your face.

00:22:29.350 --> 00:22:33.210
What's happening is
communication is the number

00:22:33.210 --> 00:22:35.340
one, two, three, and
four things that matter.

00:22:35.340 --> 00:22:37.550
I believe pretty executives in this room

00:22:37.550 --> 00:22:38.980
that what I'm talking about right now

00:22:38.980 --> 00:22:40.980
is not the kind of thing
that you go and hire

00:22:40.980 --> 00:22:44.240
an agency for or put
your niece in charge of.

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:46.150
This is something you
yourself have to spend

00:22:46.150 --> 00:22:49.320
50 to 100 hours to deeply understand

00:22:49.320 --> 00:22:51.350
because I believe being
able to communicate

00:22:51.350 --> 00:22:54.620
in a contemporary
fashion continues to grow

00:22:54.620 --> 00:22:56.770
in importance on the
outcomes of businesses

00:22:56.770 --> 00:23:00.300
and is now as equally as
important as being able

00:23:00.300 --> 00:23:02.470
to pay your taxes, balance your checkbook,

00:23:02.470 --> 00:23:05.290
and breathe oxygen within
a business environment.

00:23:05.290 --> 00:23:08.850
I believe that marketing has now become

00:23:08.850 --> 00:23:12.390
an operational function that if you really

00:23:12.390 --> 00:23:14.200
look at what's happening,

00:23:14.200 --> 00:23:18.290
it is unbelievably
impossible to be successful

00:23:18.290 --> 00:23:23.060
without having your comms strategy tight,

00:23:23.060 --> 00:23:26.060
strong, and remarkably effective.

00:23:26.060 --> 00:23:29.442
I laugh at the old adages
of, like, you know,

00:23:29.442 --> 00:23:32.210
I think it's David
Ogilvy or whatever it is,

00:23:32.210 --> 00:23:33.630
that the old adage of marketing right,

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:36.100
we spend all this money
but 50% of it I waste,

00:23:36.100 --> 00:23:38.197
I just don't know what 50%.

00:23:38.197 --> 00:23:41.160
That makes a lot of sense
to me as now as a historian

00:23:41.160 --> 00:23:43.650
of the marketing world, the
television, print, radio,

00:23:43.650 --> 00:23:46.350
that makes zero sense in 2020.

00:23:46.350 --> 00:23:49.300
I know where every penny does
everything about everything.

00:23:49.300 --> 00:23:50.930
And more importantly, this is not a sales

00:23:50.930 --> 00:23:52.950
and conversion game,
this is a branding game.

00:23:52.950 --> 00:23:55.160
Let me explain what I mean by that.

00:23:55.160 --> 00:23:57.090
Another tidbit that I
would love for this room

00:23:57.090 --> 00:23:59.190
to leave with is the following.

00:23:59.190 --> 00:24:00.840
The biggest mistake that people make

00:24:00.840 --> 00:24:02.880
when they do digital
marketing is they think

00:24:02.880 --> 00:24:04.640
of it as a sales channel.

00:24:04.640 --> 00:24:08.080
A lot of people go into deal
mode, or conversion mode,

00:24:08.080 --> 00:24:10.730
to prove its ROI, and what they're doing

00:24:10.730 --> 00:24:12.200
is they're hurting their brand,

00:24:12.200 --> 00:24:13.920
and they're training
their consumers to buy

00:24:13.920 --> 00:24:16.290
their product on a cycle which hurts

00:24:16.290 --> 00:24:18.240
all of our collective margins.

00:24:18.240 --> 00:24:20.038
If you look at what I do with my content

00:24:20.038 --> 00:24:22.350
or what other successful
businesses are doing.

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:24.970
I'm treating social as
a branding platform,

00:24:24.970 --> 00:24:26.810
not as a sales platform.

00:24:26.810 --> 00:24:27.990
The content I'm putting out there is

00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:30.240
to build brand, not sales.

00:24:30.240 --> 00:24:32.790
That is a very unique execution

00:24:32.790 --> 00:24:35.162
that I'm seeing in the marketplace.

00:24:35.162 --> 00:24:37.010
Too many people look at
TV, or naming rights,

00:24:37.010 --> 00:24:39.350
or putting your company's name on jerseys,

00:24:39.350 --> 00:24:41.120
or whatever it might
be as the branding play

00:24:41.120 --> 00:24:44.330
but digital as the
lower funnel sales play.

00:24:44.330 --> 00:24:47.390
The rep, you know the thing
you take away from the

00:24:47.390 --> 00:24:50.750
deals that you would have
done on print or direct mail.

00:24:50.750 --> 00:24:53.300
I believe that is a huge vulnerability

00:24:53.300 --> 00:24:55.210
and that what people
